Yep... the PYEC is a very special credit card, or should I say, it's
the ONLY charge card in the game!  But to answer the OPs question,
there is a timeout, much like prayer.  If you try to invoke the PYEC
before the timeout expires, time is added back to the timeout (3d10
turns, I believe).

The initial timeout is set as rnz(100), which is an ugly function:

int rnz(i)
{
tmp = 1000;
tmp += rn2(1000);
tmp *= rne(4);
if (rn2(2)) { i *= tmp; i /= 1000;}
else        { i *= 1000; i /= tmp;}
}

rn2(x) is an even distribution of integers 0 <= rn2(x) < x, or any
number from 0 to x-1.

rne(x) is quite ugly:

utmp = (u.ulevel < 15) ? 5 : u.ulevel/3;
(This is the lesser of 5 and your level/3)

tmp=1;
while(tmp<utmp && !rn2(x))
    tmp++;

So, up to utmp-1 times, there is a (x-1) in x chance tmp is
incremented, otherwise, it stops.

Thus, for a level 15 character, rne(4) produces:

max of 4 times through the while loop:  75% chance each time of adding
1 to tmp.  This produces:

Result             %Chance
(rne(4) at u.ulevel < 18)
---------------------------------
1                  25%
2                  18.75%       (75%*25%)
3                  14.0625%     (75%*75%*25%)
4                  10.546875%   (75%*75%*75%*25%)
5                  31.640625%   (75%*75%*75%*75%)

Back to rnz, we see that we started with tmp=1000, added 0-999 to that
(from rn2(1000)), then multiplied by 1-5 (from rne(4)).  This value
ranges from 1000 ((1000+0)*1) to 9995 ((1000+999)*5).

Then, there is a 50/50 chance we take i (which is 100, passed in from
artifact.c), multiply by tmp and divide by 1000, or that we multiply i
by 1000 and divide by tmp.

This is then:  100 * (1000 ... 9995) / 1000 50% of the time
          or:  100 * 1000 / (1000 ... 9995) 50% of the time

Therefore, the absolute limit for timeouts for a character up to 18th
level is 10 to 999 ( 10 = 100 * 1000/9995, 999 = (100 * 9995 / 1000)).
 This range will increase every third character level past level 18
(rne(x) can return values from 1 to u.ulevel/3).

